00:00:01.420 --> 00:00:04.810
Chris: So, what exactly is
Kanban and how does it work?

00:00:04.830 --> 00:00:06.950
Leah: Kanban first came about in Japan.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:11.710
The Japanese word ‘Kanban’ translates
as ‘signboard’ or ‘visual card’.

00:00:12.219 --> 00:00:17.810
In the 1940s it was developed [with LEAN
methodology] by an engineer for Toyota.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:21.749
It was predominately used in
‘just-in-time’ manufacturing.

00:00:22.310 --> 00:00:26.689
Kanban came to the attention of the
software industry and is nowadays

00:00:26.689 --> 00:00:28.720
used in the Agile methodology.

00:00:29.030 --> 00:00:34.870
The objective of Kanban is to simplify
planning, manage work and inventory, and

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:37.260
increase productivity and efficiency.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.250
Chris: Kanban is a visual system,
and the flow of work is managed

00:00:42.270 --> 00:00:44.129
using a Kanban project board.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.230
Often these same types of project boards
are used in Agile and Scrum methodology.

00:00:49.900 --> 00:00:52.919
In its simplest form there are
three columns which are labelled:

00:00:53.009 --> 00:00:55.530
‘To Do’, ‘In Progress’, and ‘Done’.

00:00:56.269 --> 00:00:59.390
These labels would be different
depending on the type of project,

00:00:59.740 --> 00:01:04.370
so for example a Scrum Kanban board
could have more columns and could

00:01:04.370 --> 00:01:09.969
include Stories, Current iteration,
In Progress, Tested, and Delivered.

00:01:10.630 --> 00:01:12.479
So how does this work in practice?

00:01:13.660 --> 00:01:16.510
Leah: A task is put on a
card and placed on the board.

00:01:17.000 --> 00:01:19.870
Each card has a priority
and a team member.

00:01:20.650 --> 00:01:24.560
As the task is assigned, worked
on, and completed, it is moved

00:01:24.599 --> 00:01:26.249
from one column to the next.

00:01:27.049 --> 00:01:30.280
As the cards move across the
board, you can identify issues

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:32.090
at each stage of the process.

00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:36.390
When all task cards are in the
‘Done’ column, the iteration

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:37.830
is marked as complete.

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:42.420
There are 4 principles of Kanban,
so, let’s have a look at these.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:44.459
Chris: Visualise workflow

00:01:45.630 --> 00:01:49.030
The Kanban board is the project
visual workflow as in there

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:51.170
would be three columns e.g.

00:01:51.320 --> 00:01:55.450
To do, In Progress, and Done [as
we have already mentioned], with

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.139
coloured cards for each task.

00:01:57.870 --> 00:02:00.450
These would be assigned to team
members and moved according

00:02:00.450 --> 00:02:01.600
to their current status.

00:02:02.520 --> 00:02:04.119
Leah: Limit work in progress [WIP]

00:02:05.399 --> 00:02:10.390
This is where the individual tasks would
be prioritised, assigned a certain amount

00:02:10.539 --> 00:02:15.740
of ‘points’ [time to take/complexity], and
there would be a limit on how many tasks

00:02:15.790 --> 00:02:18.299
[workload] each team member would have.

00:02:18.870 --> 00:02:20.140
Focus on Flow

00:02:20.750 --> 00:02:24.270
Chris: The workflow should run
smoothly, but stoppages could be used

00:02:24.270 --> 00:02:26.109
as opportunities for improvement.

00:02:26.730 --> 00:02:30.649
Measurements may be used to track
and analyse such as ‘Lead time’,

00:02:30.859 --> 00:02:32.829
‘cycle time’ and ‘workload’.

00:02:33.660 --> 00:02:36.170
Leah: And finally Continuous improvement

00:02:36.560 --> 00:02:40.820
The results and the Kanban system would
be monitored to determine if it works,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:43.249
and if not something else should be tried.

